## 1. Entity Relationship Diagram

*(Insert ERD image here, e.g. `docs/erd_diagram.png`)*

The ERD shows five entities: `Users`, `Transaction_Categories`, `Transactions`, `Transaction_Participants`, and `System_Logs`. Crow's-foot notation indicates cardinality between each pair of related tables.

### Design Rationale

The schema separates raw SMS ingestion from structured transaction data to preserve auditability while enabling efficient querying. `Transactions` is the central fact table, holding the numeric and temporal data needed for analytics (amount, fee, balance, datetime), with `raw_body` retained for traceability back to the source SMS.

`Users` is deliberately generic rather than split into `Senders`/`Receivers`, since the same person (e.g. "Jane Smith") appears as both across different transactions — normalizing them into one table avoids duplicate records and enables per-user transaction history. The relationship between `Transactions` and `Users` is naturally many-to-many: one transaction involves two users (sender, receiver), and one user participates in many transactions. This is resolved with the `Transaction_Participants` junction table, which also carries a `role` attribute distinguishing sender from receiver — critical since a single row can't otherwise express directionality.

`Transaction_Categories` is separated out rather than using a plain string column, since the ETL categorization step already classifies transactions into a fixed set of types (Incoming Money, Payment, Transfer, Bank Deposit, Airtime, Data Bundle), and a lookup table keeps this consistent and query-efficient (e.g. `GROUP BY category_id`).

`System_Logs` supports the ETL pipeline's error-handling path (mirroring the Logs and Dead-Letter Queue component in the system architecture), capturing SMS that failed to parse alongside successfully processed transaction logs — without forcing every SMS to become a `Transactions` row.

